  "verdict": "0. What kind of statement this is. Every omission in the model pushes tissue temperature UP, so these are UPPER bounds \u2014 they can establish 'not shown to be safe', never 'the tissue dies'. The verdict is strong because of the MARGIN, not the direction: the baseline cambium sits 68 \u00b0C above the 50 \u00b0C gate and stays above it on every sweep point. 1. Cambium: the procedure as the canon writes it (uniform induction heating of the Ti) crosses the coagulation gate after 111 s and reaches 118 \u00b0C by the end of the 23 min hold. The CROSSING is the duration-free result; the peak and every wound diameter are still rising at the end, so they are monotone in a hold length inherited from the very 1D model this script supersedes. 2. There is no window in which the resin is cauterised and the cambium is spared: with the whole Ti pinned at 200 \u00b0C the channel wall passes the coagulation point at 5 s and the cambium follows at 111 s \u2014 both inside the first two minutes, after which only the damage keeps growing. 3. Measured in the unit the canon already uses: killed LIVING tissue reaches \u00d848 mm against the mechanical \u00d815 mm, and the cambial ring alone is \u00d844 mm \u2014 which under the same 4 % CODIT rule that yields 'DBH >= 38 cm' would demand DBH >= 111 cm. \u26a0\ufe0f Carrying that rule from a drilled wound to a thermal ring is an assumption about how a tree walls off dead cambium over intact wood, not something measured here. 4. Axial reach: heating only the accessible metal leaves the anode at 21 \u00b0C \u2014 the 50 mm PEEK break does exactly what 01_01 \u00a74.1 designed it for. The 1D cache could not see this because it modelled a solid Ti rod, and not because it predated the pivot: the three-zone anchor was canon 12 days earlier. 5. \ud83d\udd34 No variant is clean, and the cambium-only metric hid it. Selective deep heating spares the cambium (48 \u00b0C) but kills living tissue out to \u00d848 mm \u2014 WIDER than the \u00d844 mm cambial ring that condemns the canon variant. Pre-heated insertion spares both (44 \u00b0C, \u00d818 mm) and cauterises nothing (wall peaks at 80 \u00b0C). 6. The one lever that is cheap and untested: a 2 mm still-air isolator under the flange takes the cambium from 118 to 109 \u00b0C and killed living tissue from \u00d848 to \u00d848 mm. \u26a0\ufe0f It must be a GAS gap: dead periderm already conducts at 0.10 and PEEK at 0.25, so a PEEK washer would improve the path to the cambium rather than block it. 7. Field equipment: holding the canon procedure needs 20 W of sustained induction into the anchor at steady state \u2014 a number the canon never states and the bench has to supply.",
  "ceilings": "Conduction only (no sap advection, no evaporation, no charring); zero contact resistance; isotherm damage criterion, NOT a time-temperature dose; bark/phloem thickness and cp_Ti have no SSOT home and are swept/derived; gyroid lambda_eff is an estimator band, not a measurement. The wood-wall probe sits h/2 outside the anchor surface (0.25 mm at the default grid), so a zero dwell there means the coagulated layer is thinner than the grid, not absent. Every other listed omission biases tissue temperature UPWARD, so the cambium verdict is conservative."
}